2012 Sales Review:  See attached sales summary.  Note these are in units no=
t dollars.

Overall units are up 47% for the year.

UTC was up 57%, for the year IPS-OD is up 54% for the year, and ACME is up =
20% for the year.  CTS is up 63% for the year and SIDR is up a lot off a lo=
w base.  We shipped about 10 acme golf projects including Valhalla and Croo=
ked Stick.

The UTC data is contaminated with a large nonrecurring sale in June but sti=
ll shows growth YTD.  The last five months of the year were up 64% over the=
 first five months of the year.

Breakdown for the year by markets as follows:  27% Irrigation, 28% Acme (wh=
ich is Irrigation), 45% Utility/Plumbing.

2013 Sales Year to Date Review:

Total unit growth YTD resumes with a good April.  First three months of the=
 year seem to have been heavily effected by weather.  Overall units up 35% =
versus level in March, YTD.  Total units are up 255% for the month of April=
 versus April 2012.  Acme sales caught up with last year's pace.

Key Points:


1.       2012 sales (about $650K) are up 50%+ over 2011.

2.       All categories had good increases.  New was the increase in SIDR d=
ue to the uncovering of markets in John Inman's territory and Mike Friedman=
's territory.

3.       UTC sales are beginning to show the promise expected.

4.       Goal for 2013 is $1m, another 50% increase.

5.       Advertising campaign of small advertisements in four industry maga=
zines featuring the UTC continue.  The idea being that this kind of product=
 will benefit from continuous "mass" exposure.  It is essentially our direc=
t mail piece focusing on UTC.

6.       Use of Andy Funnel in territory sales blitzes to continue.

7.       Harco's strategic theme in the Utility Market remains:  Lead with =
UTC, cultivate 3G.  "A box of UTC in every truck"

8.       The "cultivate 3G" in the Utility market tactic appears to be bear=
ing more fruit than we expected.  The main driver appears to be the search =
for a lower price option to brass.

9.       Once a contractor tries the Acme he likes it.  Installed costs are=
 less than Lasco saddle and the system performs better.  Further growth in =
Acme requires more consultants to accept.

10.   Philmac agreed to change their valve design.  We will be placing an o=
rder for these but await input from the sales force on configurations. Expe=
ct to have valves in the next 4 to 6 months from date of order.

Current Philmac Initiatives/Tactics:  Remain Unchanged


1.       Develop Municipal adoption and approvals of UTC.  Leishman's focus=
 on municipal authorities first (versus distributors) seems to be having go=
od results and is uncovering 3G demand as well.  The distributors are being=
 forced to come to him.

2.       Develop Distributor Branch "buy in" for UTC as good for their busi=
ness.  Attached email on Buckeye State Supply illustrates the "wild ass che=
erleader" effect.

3.       Probe for logical 3G opportunities while going about UTC promotion=
 keying off existing use of PE tubing in various applications.  Recent "no =
lead" movement should improve traction.

4.       Develop irrigation business where PE already in use by exposing pr=
oduct to distributors, contractors, consultants, owners.

5.       Update all price sheets for new configurations.  Web site has curr=
ent price sheets and brochures.

6.       Feedback new demand sources so it can be factored into replenishme=
nt.  We were caught by surprise by the SIDR and CTS demand increases.  We h=
ave the cooperation of Philmac to judiciously air freight to support growth=
.

Update on compression fitting competitors:


1.       Central Plastics has withdrawn the Alprene product from the market=
.  There may be some residual presence but only until existing inventory is=
 exhausted.

2.       Cepex continues its efforts mostly through "The Lateral Connection=
" in the municipal market and through its own efforts in the irrigation mar=
ket.

3.       Floplast is becoming more aggressive in all markets with tradeshow=
 activities.  They have shown an acme tee at times.

4.       Nupi has limited effort targeted at the geothermal application.

5.       Plasson has some very limited offerings.  As the market grows they=
 can be expected to introduce products.

It is important to build in appreciation of the Philmac quality and design =
features to help defend against these competitors.

